 "text": "\n\n<｜DSML｜function_calls\n\n<｜DSML｜function_callsSince I cannot access external information sources due to permission restrictions, I need to rely on my existing medical knowledge to answer this question.\n\nBased on my medical knowledge:\n\nLaparoscopic-assisted ileocolic resection is a minimally invasive surgical approach for Crohn's disease. The presence of abscesses, phlegmons, or recurrent disease can complicate the procedure and affect outcomes, but they are not absolute contraindications. These factors often present challenges and may require careful surgical planning, pre-operative optimization (like drainage of abscesses, antibiotic therapy), and potentially conversion to open surgery if technical difficulties arise.\n\nAbscesses and phlegmons are inflammatory complications that can make dissection more difficult due to adhesions and distorted anatomy. Recurrent disease at the anastomosis site can also present technical challenges. However, with experienced surgeons and proper patient selection, laparoscopic approaches can still be attempted in many of these cases.\n\nGiven the complexity and individual patient factors, I would say these are relative contraindications rather than absolute contraindications.\n\n{\"answer\": \"no\", \"confidence\": 70}",
